Subject: [PATCH 07950/11362] [outer-loop] fix CONTROL CONTAMINATION: every
 explorer arm now pins all seven gated flags

An arm pinning only a SUBSET of the gated flags inherits module defaults for the rest, so the moment
a flag is flipped that arm's meaning changes underneath already-published numbers. It happened:
  arm A  pinned 2/7  -> after today's flips it stopped being 'the pre-flip agent'
  arm B2 pinned 3/7  -> the HUD A/B's own CONTROL became the HUD TREATMENT
Retrospectively the published runs are unaffected (they executed BEFORE the flips, when the
inherited defaults were the intended values), but any FUTURE A/B using A or B2 as a control would
have measured a contaminated control. That made this a PREREQUISITE for the queued inert-click-pruner
A/B, not cleanup.

All 11 explorer arms now pin 7/7 (E is exempt and asserted to take no explorer kwargs -- it is the
just-explore reference shim and constructs no StepwiseExplorer). This is a PINNING change, not a
redefinition: each arm's measured semantics are preserved, verified behaviourally --
  A  -> tier=False unif=False hud=False guard=False   (the pre-flip agent)
  B2 -> tier=True  unif=True  hud=False guard=False   (what it was as the HUD A/B's control)
  G3 -> tier=True  unif=True  hud=True  guard=True    (the flip candidate)
The reconstruction was independently validated by the convention-perturbation battery
(scripts/experiments/cptb_arms.py), whose explicitly-pinned pre-flip control reproduces arm A's
historical win set {cd82, lf52, lp85, sp80, su15, tu93, vc33} exactly.

Two new tests make the drift class impossible to reintroduce quietly: adding a new gated flag, or a
new arm, now FAILS until every arm pins it; and a second test asserts the preserved semantics so a
future 'pinning' edit cannot silently redefine A, B2 or G3.

Also fixed one brittle exact-dict assertion on arm D's kwargs (same class as the 'tier' annotation
breakage) -- now asserts D's DEFINING values (barrier on + greedy draw + gradient) instead.
148 targeted tests pass.

